Vlad Popescu <dndfanREMOVETHIS@hotpop.com.this.too> wrote:

>I've skimmed through the guidebook (enough to write my own nethackrc), 
>but I still haven't found any hints on how one can handle the 
>following objects: altars, statues, fountains and sinks. 

There are many spoilers online which can provide you with more details
about everything you like to know. In the game, ask the Oracle for major
consultations.

>I have tried praying at the altar, but I usually end up getting my god angry.

Praying can help you out of trouble in any situation. To create holy
water, drop potions of water onto a coaligned altar and pray.

>Sacrificing a jackal corpse also didn't do much (I tried this only once,
>worshipping Kos). 

Gods don't like old corpses.

Let's here the Oracle about altars: "The detection of blessings and
curses is in the domain of the gods.  They will make this information
available to mortals who request it at their places of worship, or
elsewhere for those mortals who devote themselves to the service
of the gods."

>Onward to statues... I have absolutely no idea what to do with them. Any hints
>are appreciated.

Statues created by stoning monsters contain the monster's inventory.
Statues generated by the game may contain spellbooks.

>When I stumble upon a fountain or sink, I drink from it (not the smartest thing
>to do most of the time). What else can I do with them?

Again, we consult the Oracle: "The cliche of the kitchen sink swallowing
any unfortunate rings that contact its pernicious surface reflecteth
greater truth than many homilies, yet even so, few have developed the
skill to identify enchanted rings by the transfigurations effected upon
the voracious device's frame."

>Finally, I end up losing or accidentally killing my pet most of the time 
>(actually quite heartbreaking, beacause I named the cat after my real-life pet
>cat). How can I 'train' pets (so I've read around the newsgroup).

You can tame cats, dogs and horses by throwing certain food items at
them. There are also magical ways to tame monsters; some obvious (such as
scrolls), some not so (such as certain effects of magical traps).
